"""Builds the LPS document: SERS spectrum compared with the lipid A simulation (and bulk LPS)."""
import os,pickle
import fr_docs as FD
S=r"C:\Users\sukes\AppData\Local\Temp\claude\C--Users-sukes-Downloads-sukesh\f9c0734c-e0bb-4f52-a4e5-d9f33e9bce00\scratchpad\sersfinal"
Rz=pickle.load(open(S+r'\lps_res.pkl','rb')); res=Rz['res']; f1,f2,f3,f4=Rz['figs']
OUT=os.path.join(FD.OUT,'08_LPS_SERS_vs_Simulation'); os.makedirs(OUT,exist_ok=True)
n_ag=sum(r['status']=='agrees' for r in res); n_sh=sum(r['status']=='shifted' for r in res); n_no=sum(r['status']=='none' for r in res)
typed=[r for r in res if r['typed']]
d=FD.Doc('LPS: SERS Spectrum Compared with the Molecular Simulation','Surface-enhanced Raman bands of lipopolysaccharide on etched silicon and the calculated spectrum of the lipid A model')
d.h1('Executive summary')
hi=[r for r in res if r['delta'] is not None and r['delta']<0]; mt=[r for r in res if r['calc'] is not None]
d.p(f"A reproducible set of ten bands was found in the SERS spectra of LPS on etched silicon (on {Rz['n']} of 35 LPS spots). These bands were compared with the visible peaks of the calculated Raman spectrum of the whole-molecule lipid A model ({Rz['ncp']} peaks between 400 and 1800 cm⁻¹) and with the bulk LPS reference spectrum. Only {n_ag} of the ten SERS bands has a calculated peak within 10 cm⁻¹ (1330 cm⁻¹, calculated 1338 cm⁻¹). {n_sh} further bands have a calculated peak 11 to 30 cm⁻¹ away, and the band at 1592 cm⁻¹ has no calculated peak within 50 cm⁻¹. In {len(hi)} of the {len(mt)} matched bands the calculated peak lies at higher wavenumber than the SERS band.")
d.p(f"The two bands with a published assignment, 1330 cm⁻¹ (C–H deformation) and 1455 cm⁻¹ (CH₂/CH₃ deformation), both have a calculated C–H bending peak of the same type nearby (1338 and 1466 cm⁻¹), so the vibration type agrees while the position is shifted by 8 and 11 cm⁻¹. For the other bands no vibration type is assigned in the literature, so the shifts cannot be judged by type. The agreement is no better than chance: when the calculated peak positions are displaced at random by 15 to 70 cm⁻¹, {Rz['dec_mean']:.1f} of the ten bands on average fall within 10 cm⁻¹ of a calculated peak, against {Rz['real']} for the real positions (p = {Rz['p']:.2f}). The overall shape does not match either (correlation {Rz['r_calc']:.2f} with the calculation and {Rz['r_bulk']:.2f} with the bulk LPS spectrum), and none of the bulk LPS peaks, including the strongest at 425 cm⁻¹, appears in the SERS spectra. The SERS pattern is consistent with CH₂ and CH₃ groups of an organic layer on the silicon, with LPS as one possible source; it is not an identification.")
d.h1('Objective')
d.p("To test whether the bands seen in the SERS spectra of LPS correspond to vibrations predicted for the molecular structure of lipid A, the conserved core of LPS, and to explain bands that are shifted. SERS (surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy) is Raman spectroscopy in which molecules on a roughened metal-coated surface give a strongly amplified signal; it often enhances different bands than ordinary Raman spectroscopy of the bulk powder, so the SERS and bulk spectra of one molecule do not have to look alike.")
d.h1('Data and approach')
d.b("Ten bands that repeat on 21 of 35 LPS spots (mean spectrum of those spots; positions are the means of the spots). Spectra were processed with background subtraction, spike removal, smoothing and baseline removal, and scaled to the silicon peak at 518 cm⁻¹.","SERS data:")
d.b("A whole-molecule model of lipid A with two Kdo sugars (201 atoms, charge −4, with shortened fatty-acid chains), calculated for the isolated molecule with density functional theory (B3LYP, small 3-21G basis set). A calculated spectrum is a prediction of the Raman signals expected from the structure. Frequencies were multiplied by 0.965 and each vibration was drawn as a peak 12 cm⁻¹ wide. The type of each vibration (for example C–H bending, C–O and C–C stretching, skeletal deformation) was assigned from which atoms move; these labels are tentative.","Simulation:")
d.b("No spectrum was shifted or rescaled to improve the match; each is scaled to its own maximum, and the silicon region is left out. The calculated peaks are the visible maxima of the broadened calculated spectrum (height above the surrounding curve of at least 0.05 on a 0 to 1 scale), each given the vibration type of its strongest vibration. Each SERS band is matched to the nearest calculated peak within 50 cm⁻¹. For bands with a published assignment (1009, 1330 and 1455 cm⁻¹) the calculated peak must also be of the matching type; for the other seven bands no vibration type is assigned in the literature (two of them, 785 and 1592 cm⁻¹, lie at or near peaks listed as common to commercial endotoxins without an assignment), so the match is by position only. 'Agrees' means a difference of 10 cm⁻¹ or less; 'shifted' means 10 to 50 cm⁻¹.","Comparison:")
d.h1('Results')
d.h2('The SERS spectrum of LPS')
d.figure(f1,"Mean SERS spectrum of the LPS spots that carry the band set (red line, with ± 1 standard deviation as the shaded band), scaled to the height of the silicon peak. Each number marks a band at its position on the curve.")
d.p("The strongest band is at 1455 cm⁻¹; the others are weaker and several are broad or overlapping (1330 and 1368 cm⁻¹ sit on one broad feature).")
d.h2('SERS, simulation and bulk spectrum together')
d.figure(f2,"Bulk LPS reference (top), SERS mean spectrum (middle) and calculated spectrum of the lipid A model (bottom). In each panel the dots and numbers mark only the peaks of that curve, at the position where they occur on it: the bulk peaks (top), the SERS bands (middle) and the visible peaks of the calculated spectrum (bottom). The calculated peaks marked are the visible maxima of the calculated spectrum. Numbers on the curves are read from the plotted curve (the apex of the mean spectrum) and can differ by a few cm⁻¹ from the band positions in the table, which are averages over individual spots. Grey bands: silicon peak (middle) and glass zone (top, bottom), which are not compared.")
d.p("None of the bulk LPS peaks (425, 766, 862, 996 and 1080 cm⁻¹) appears in the SERS spectrum. The strongest calculated peaks (near 1512, 1314, 993 and 948 cm⁻¹) are not the strongest SERS bands either: the strongest SERS band, 1455 cm⁻¹, falls between calculated peaks at 1414 and 1466 cm⁻¹, and the calculated 1512 cm⁻¹ peak, the strongest in the calculation, has no strong SERS counterpart (the nearest SERS band is 1536 cm⁻¹).")
d.h2('Band-by-band comparison')
ST={'agrees':'Agrees','shifted':'Shifted','none':'Not reproduced'}
LITO={785:'794, listed as a common LPS peak without assignment (9 cm⁻¹ away)',1592:'1592, listed as a common LPS peak without assignment'}
rows=[]
for r in res:
    typ='' if r['typed'] else ' (type not assigned)'
    rows.append([f"{r['exp']:.0f}",(f"{r['calc']:.0f}" if r['calc'] else '—'),(f"{r['delta']:+.0f}" if r['delta'] is not None else '—'),(r['label'] if r['label'] else '—'),r['desc'] if r['typed'] else LITO.get(r['key'],'none in the literature checked'),ST[r['status']]+typ])
d.table(['SERS band (cm⁻¹)','Calculated (cm⁻¹)','Difference','Calculated vibration','Published assignment','Result'],rows,[1.9,2.0,1.7,4.2,4.4,2.6],'SERS bands of LPS and the corresponding calculated vibrations. Difference = SERS minus calculated.')
d.figure(f3,"Close-ups of the SERS bands. Upper curve: SERS mean; lower curve: calculation; each is scaled to its tallest peak in the window. Dashed lines mark the SERS band (above) and the corresponding calculated vibration (below); the arrow gives the shift where it exceeds 10 cm⁻¹.")
d.p("Each close-up shows the SERS band (upper curve) and the nearest calculated peak (lower curve). The calculated peaks are mostly broader and lie to the high-wavenumber side of the SERS band; at 1592 cm⁻¹ there is no calculated peak.")
d.h2('Shifted bands: same peak region, different position')
sh=[x for x in res if x['status']=='shifted']
d.p("A SERS band and a calculated peak that are 11 to 30 cm⁻¹ apart are not identical in position. They are treated as possibly the same vibration only where the calculated peak is of the expected type or, for bands with no assigned type, where it is the nearest calculated peak; the following list gives each case:")
for r in sorted(sh,key=lambda r:r['exp']):
    extra=" The calculated 1338 cm⁻¹ peak also serves the 1330 cm⁻¹ band, so one calculated peak stands for two SERS bands." if r['exp']>1360 and r['exp']<1370 else ""
    d.b(f"SERS {r['exp']:.0f} cm⁻¹, calculated {r['calc']:.0f} cm⁻¹ ({r['delta']:+.0f} cm⁻¹), {r['label']}"+(" (type matches the published assignment)." if r['typed'] else " (position only; the type is not assigned in the experiment).")+extra)
d.p("Likely reasons for the offsets (not separately tested): the calculation is for an isolated molecule with a small basis set, and the single frequency scale factor (0.965) was not fitted for that level, so a systematic offset of 10 to 25 cm⁻¹ is expected; on the silicon surface the molecule binds, hydrogen bonds and takes a preferred orientation, which shifts bands and changes which are enhanced; and the calculated spectrum comes from a shortened lipid A, not the whole LPS. The shifts are mostly in one direction (the calculated peak is higher), which points to the method rather than to different vibrations, but this does not make the assignment secure.")

d.h2('Where the matched vibrations are in the molecule')
d.figure(f4,"Atoms that move most in the strongest vibration of each calculated peak that was matched to a SERS band. Each colour is one vibration (key at right); hydrogen atoms are assigned to the atom they are bonded to. The vibrations are spread over several groups rather than one bond.")
d.h1('Interpretation')
d.b(f"Positions: compared with the visible peaks of the calculation, only the 1330 cm⁻¹ band is within 10 cm⁻¹ of a calculated peak (1338 cm⁻¹); the others are shifted by 11 to 30 cm⁻¹ or, for 1592 cm⁻¹, have no calculated peak. Weak individual calculated vibrations lie closer to several SERS bands, but they do not form visible peaks and would be a misleading basis for a match.")
d.b(f"Chance: with {Rz['ncp']} calculated peaks over 1400 cm⁻¹, {Rz['dec_mean']:.1f} of the ten bands fall within 10 cm⁻¹ of a calculated peak on average when positions are displaced at random, against {Rz['real']} for the real positions (p = {Rz['p']:.2f}). The agreement in position is therefore not evidence that the bands belong to LPS.")
d.b("Vibration type: the two bands with a published assignment, 1330 cm⁻¹ and 1455 cm⁻¹, have a calculated C–H bending peak of the same type nearby (1338 and 1466 cm⁻¹). This is consistent with CH₂/CH₃ and C–H groups, which occur in LPS (fatty-acid chains and sugars) and in many other organic layers.")
d.b("Pattern: the SERS spectrum does not reproduce the bulk LPS peaks or the calculated intensity pattern. This can happen because SERS enhances only the groups close to or lying on the metal surface, but it also removes the support a match would have given.")
d.b("Sample controls: there was no blank or solvent-only control recorded in the same period as the LPS spots, so contamination from preparation or handling cannot be excluded.")
d.h1('Conclusion and next step')
d.p("The SERS bands of the LPS spots match the calculated lipid A spectrum only loosely: one band lies within 10 cm⁻¹ of a calculated peak, the others are shifted by 11 to 30 cm⁻¹ or have no counterpart, and the agreement is not stronger than chance. The vibration type agrees for the two bands that have a published assignment. The bands are therefore not attributed to LPS. The most useful next steps are to repeat the LPS measurement on a fresh substrate with a solvent-only control prepared in the same way and a blank at the start and end of the set, and to measure bulk LPS on the same type of silicon to see which bands LPS gives there. On the calculation side, a larger basis set with water and counter-ions would test whether the offsets of 10 to 25 cm⁻¹ come from the method, and a model with the molecule bound to a surface would test the enhancement pattern.")
path=os.path.join(OUT,'LPS_SERS_vs_Simulation_Comparison.docx'); d.save(path); print(path)